parts.body:
A producer may not use a [[senior-designation|senior-specific certification or designation]] in a way that misleads buyers into thinking he or she has special training in advising seniors. Prohibited uses include a designation that is:

- not actually earned, or that the producer is ineligible to use;
- nonexistent or **self-conferred**;
- implying qualifications the producer doesn't have; or
- from an organization primarily engaged in **sales or marketing instruction**, or lacking reasonable standards for competency, discipline or continuing education.

Words such as 'senior,' 'retirement' or 'elder' combined with 'certified,' 'specialist,' 'advisor,' 'consultant' or 'planner' signal a senior-specific designation. Violations are unfair or deceptive acts and practices (§ 225.3).

**Premium paid with the application.** The producer gives a [[conditional-receipt|conditional receipt]]. Coverage is effective as of the application date (or exam date, if later) **if** the applicant is insurable as applied for under the insurer's rules.

**No premium with the application.** Coverage generally begins at [[policy-delivery|delivery]], when the producer collects the first premium and a signed [[statement-of-good-health|statement of good health]] confirming the insured's health hasn't changed.

**At delivery** the producer should:

- review the policy with the owner (coverage, riders, exclusions);
- explain any changes — a policy issued other than as applied for (for example, with a rating) is a **counteroffer** the applicant accepts by signing an **amendment**;
- collect any premium due;
- provide required disclosures such as the policy summary, and remind the owner of the free-look right.
